  "notes": "CREDENTIAL HONESTY: The University of Louisville does NOT offer a degree literally named 'BS in Computer Science'. This plan is the university's genuine official term-by-term 'Flight Plan' for the Bachelor of Science in Computer Science and Engineering (Academic Plan Code CECSBCC), J.B. Speed School of Engineering, Department of Computer Science and Engineering. (A separate BA in Computer Science exists in the College of Arts & Sciences.) The degree/major fields use UofL's TRUE program name; no substitution was made silently. SOURCE: official UofL undergraduate catalog PDF (Summer 2026-Spring 2027 catalog year), transcribed term-by-term from the 'Flight Plan' table. TRANSCRIPTION: downloaded the official catalog PDF and extracted text (pdftotext); every term's courses and credit hours were hand-verified against the PDF and the term-hour totals (16+17+13+15+1+12+1+17+1+16+15) sum to exactly 124, matching the catalog's stated 'Minimum Total Hours 124'. CAVEATS: (1) The catalog states the Flight Plan 'is intended to demonstrate one possible path to completing the degree within four years' — course selection and placement may vary by offering, electives, study abroad/internships; students consult an advisor. (2) The Flight Plan does not publish explicit per-course prerequisites, so all prereq arrays are left empty rather than inferred; the term order implies progression. (3) The four 'General Education: Cardinal Core AH, SB, or SBH' rows are student-choice slots (choose any approved Arts & Humanities, Social & Behavioral Sciences, or SB Historical Perspective course). (4) The four 'CSE Elective' rows are student-choice slots from the catalog's approved list (CSE 522/528/530/538/545/547/551/555/564/565/566/568/570/590/593; additional CSE 5XX/6XX with departmental consent). (5) CSE 412 or ECE 412 and COMM 111 or COMM 112 are either/or choice slots. (6) CSE 288 is a required 0-credit co-op seminar (required_zero). (7) Catalog admission notes: Speed School requires HS calculus or precalculus plus chemistry; ACT composite+math 25 (SAT 1200, math 590) with 3.0 GPA, or ACT 24 (SAT 1160, math 570) with 3.5 GPA; students without ACT/SAT need 3.0 HS GPA plus transcript/resume review. Students completing ENGL 105 in lieu of ENGL 101/102 satisfy the Written Communication requirement but may need an additional 3-hr Writing (WR) course for program credit hours. (8) Candidates for the BS must be in good standing (university GPA >= 2.25) with >= 2.25 GPA across degree-requirement courses.",
